From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Domenico D'Arrigo <d.darrigo@outlook.it>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 07:19:19 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] soc/intel/cannonlake: Reset the setup options only when the
 CMOS was lost too

The setup options are reset when RTC_PWR_STS says that the RTC lost
power. On Cannon Point that bit, GEN_PMCON_B bit 2, is read-write: on a
ThinkCentre M920x under Linux, writing 1 to it reads back 1
(0x00062210 -> 0x00062214) and writing 0 clears it. The OS, or any root
process, could then reset every option at the next boot, BIOS Lock and
Firmware protection among them.

With CMOS_POST, which the M920x builds have, also require that the CMOS
lost its contents, as the W541 does since patch 0256 of ouroBIOS: the
bootblock then finds no valid bank mark and clears both banks
(cmos_post_init()), so the previous boot reads as code 0 with no extra.
A real power loss of the RTC well does that; the bit set by software
leaves the code of the boot before.

Clear a bit set without a lost CMOS in romstage, in the register and in
the power state snapshot that rtc_init() reads through
soc_get_rtc_failed(): rtc_init() would take it for a power loss and zero
the CMOS, CMOS_POST included, and since pmc_final() clears the bit only
after a reset of the options, the next boot would find the CMOS lost
with the bit still set and reset them. A real loss loses the bank mark
too, and keeps the bit.

Software that also rewrites the CMOS bytes of CMOS_POST can still do
it: the check is against a stray bit, not against root.

TEST=On a ThinkCentre M920x with v1.6.3-rc2 of ouroBIOS: the bit set
from Linux and a reboot logged "Setup options: not reset, RTC power lost
but the CMOS kept the last boot (POST 0xf8/0x00000000)", cleared the bit
and kept the 36 options; the CMOS was not zeroed and the next boot had
no such line. A real CMOS clear not run yet.
---
 src/soc/intel/cannonlake/romstage/romstage.c | 35 +++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 34 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/soc/intel/cannonlake/romstage/romstage.c b/src/soc/intel/cannonlake/romstage/romstage.c
index f6aeda71eda..fb2a5b50f9f 100644
--- a/src/soc/intel/cannonlake/romstage/romstage.c
+++ b/src/soc/intel/cannonlake/romstage/romstage.c
@@ -3,6 +3,7 @@
 #include <arch/romstage.h>
 #include <cbmem.h>
 #include <console/console.h>
+#include <device/mmio.h>
 #include <drivers/efi/option.h>
 #include <fsp/util.h>
 #include <intelblocks/cfg.h>
@@ -10,6 +11,7 @@
 #include <intelblocks/pmclib.h>
 #include <intelblocks/smbus.h>
 #include <memory_info.h>
+#include <post.h>
 #include <soc/intel/common/smbios.h>
 #include <soc/iomap.h>
 #include <soc/pci_devs.h>
@@ -122,13 +124,44 @@ static void save_dimm_info(void)
 /*
  * The RTC lost power (a CMOS clear): reset the setup options too, which live
  * in SMMSTORE and not in the CMOS. Before FSP-M, which reads them.
+ *
+ * On Cannon Point the OS can set RTC_PWR_STS too (GEN_PMCON_B bit 2 is
+ * read-write, checked on a ThinkCentre M920x): the bit alone would let software
+ * reset every option at the next boot. With CMOS_POST the deletion also needs
+ * the CMOS to have lost its contents: the bootblock then found no valid bank
+ * mark and cleared both banks (cmos_post_init()), so the previous boot reads as
+ * code 0 with no extra. Any boot that ran before left a code there, so a reset
+ * between the deletion and pmc_final(), which clears the bit, does not repeat
+ * it either.
  */
-static bool reset_options_on_rtc_failure(const struct chipset_power_state *ps)
+static bool reset_options_on_rtc_failure(struct chipset_power_state *ps)
 {
 	if (!CONFIG(DRIVERS_EFI_RESET_OPTIONS_ON_RTC_FAILURE) ||
 	    !(ps->gen_pmcon_b & RTC_BATTERY_DEAD))
 		return false;
 
+	if (CONFIG(CMOS_POST)) {
+		u32 extra;
+		u8 code;
+
+		cmos_post_previous_boot(&code, &extra);
+		if (code || extra) {
+			printk(BIOS_WARNING, "Setup options: not reset, RTC power lost but the "
+			       "CMOS kept the last boot (POST 0x%02x/0x%08x)\n", code, extra);
+			/*
+			 * Clear the stray bit, in the register and in the snapshot
+			 * that rtc_init() reads: it would take it for a power loss
+			 * and zero the CMOS, CMOS_POST included, so that the next
+			 * boot finds the CMOS lost with the bit still set (only a
+			 * reset of the options clears it) and resets them. A real
+			 * loss loses the bank mark too, and keeps the bit.
+			 */
+			clrbits8(pmc_mmio_regs() + GEN_PMCON_B, RTC_BATTERY_DEAD);
+			ps->gen_pmcon_b &= ~RTC_BATTERY_DEAD;
+			return false;
+		}
+	}
+
 	return efi_option_reset_all() == CB_SUCCESS;
 }
 
